<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0136f94ba1f5> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[446] Alex Cranz with Gizmodo tested out Face ID and confirmed what a lot of us have probably already suspected: most of the time, Face ID works perfectly." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "446" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/0662ba3fee1f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[446] Alex Cranz with Gizmodo tested out Face ID and confirmed what a lot of us have probably already suspected: most of the time, Face ID works perfectly." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "446" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/1f23a4718b5d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[442] The new system, called Face ID, utilizes a new TrueDepth camera, which projects 30k \"invisible dots\" onto a user's face creating a 3D model that verifies identity and unlocks the screen." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "442" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/26058d28cd5b> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[442] When Apple announced its $1k iPhone X back in September, we learned they were ditching the home screen button log-in for something snazzier: facial recognition." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "442" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/29a5e1cc5281> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[446] In the video posted by Mashable, two pairs of identical twin bothers put the iPhone X's Face ID to good use." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "446" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/2ec39f840f51> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[446] In the video posted by Mashable, two pairs of identical twin bothers put the iPhone X's Face ID to good use." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "446" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/320f90259750> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[445] As noted by 9to5Mac, Apple has stated that Face ID is an even more secure way to unlock an iPhone than Touch ID was, with there being a one in 1,000,000 chance of a different person being able to dupe its facial recognition software." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "445" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/33b3555f8999> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[447] While Apple's Face ID might be making headlines, Windows Hello is a facial recognition system that has been available on Windows devices for years." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "447" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/346a494d74fe> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[445] As noted by 9to5Mac, Apple has stated that Face ID is an even more secure way to unlock an iPhone than Touch ID was, with there being a one in 1,000,000 chance of a different person being able to dupe its facial recognition software." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "445" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3856be9e78a2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[445] As noted by 9to5Mac, Apple has stated that Face ID is an even more secure way to unlock an iPhone than Touch ID was, with there being a one in 1,000,000 chance of a different person being able to dupe its facial recognition software." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "445" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3bbdf6355c21> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "It's worth noting that Apple itself admitted that Face ID may not be able to distinguish between identical twins during the iPhone X unveiling on September 12." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "448" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/3fa0522fafe0> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[442] The new system, called Face ID, utilizes a new TrueDepth camera, which projects 30k \"invisible dots\" onto a user's face creating a 3D model that verifies identity and unlocks the screen." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "442" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4a5452334916> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[446] Alex Cranz with Gizmodo tested out Face ID and confirmed what a lot of us have probably already suspected: most of the time, Face ID works perfectly." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "446" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/53465b881c90> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[442] The new system, called Face ID, utilizes a new TrueDepth camera, which projects 30k \"invisible dots\" onto a user's face creating a 3D model that verifies identity and unlocks the screen." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "442" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5eb679819e20> a airo:Evidence ;
